# Copy to .env and edit. Only one value here has to change.
#
#   cp .env.example .env

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 🔑 THE ONE THAT MATTERS: this machine's LAN address.
#
# The robot connects to it, so "localhost" and "127.0.0.1" are both wrong - they
# mean the robot itself. Find it with `ip addr` (Linux/WSL) or `ipconfig`
# (Windows) and use the address on the same network as your Wi-Fi.
#
# ⚠️ It goes in TWO places: here, and `server.websocket` / `server.vision_explain`
#    in config.yaml. The server hands its own address to the robot, so a
#    mismatch produces a robot that connects once and then cannot talk.
#
# ⚠️ And give it a DHCP reservation. When this address changes the robot stops
#    working with no error anyone can see.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
HOST_LAN_IP=192.0.2.10

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which extras to start. Ships as `gpu-nvidia`, which runs Ollama in a container
# with the GPU passed through.
#
# 🔴 LEAVE IT EMPTY IF YOU ARE NOT ON NVIDIA. A Mac cannot pass a GPU into a
#    container, and nor can Windows with an AMD card - and the request is a hard
#    failure rather than a slow fallback, so the stack will not start at all.
#    Run Ollama natively instead and point the config at it:
#
#      COMPOSE_PROFILES=
#      then in data/.config.yaml, under the Ollama entry:
#        base_url: http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1
#
#    docs/your-llm.md has the whole thing per platform, and the one command that
#    tells you whether your GPU is actually being used.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
COMPOSE_PROFILES=gpu-nvidia

# Your timezone, so his clock and any time-shaped answers are right.
TZ=UTC

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The model. `ollama pull` it first, or use ./use-model.sh which does both:
#
#   ./use-model.sh qwen3:14b
#
# Sizes, measured - see docs/model-floor.md before choosing:
#   qwen3:14b   a good default on 12 GB+ - reliable tool calls
#   qwen3:8b    the smallest that behaves; fits 8 GB
#   qwen3:4b    documented so you do not have to find out yourself
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
LLM_MODEL=qwen3:14b

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Speech recognition. English-only on purpose - see the note in the compose file
# before changing it to a multilingual model.
#   small.en   good default
#   base.en    faster, noticeably worse on names
#   medium.en  better, and heavy on CPU
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHISPER_MODEL=Systran/faster-whisper-small.en

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: llama.cpp instead of Ollama, for the extra tokens per second.
# Uncomment both, put a GGUF in MODELS_DIR, and set selected_module.LLM to
# LlamaCpp in the config.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=llamacpp
#MODELS_DIR=/home/you/models
#LLAMACPP_MODEL_FILE=Qwen3-14B-Q4_K_M.gguf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Advanced: which interface the robot's two ports bind to. The default listens
# on everything, which is what you want on a home network. Narrow it to your LAN
# address if this machine is on a network you do not control.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0
